From fe1a466d1bfef90392d6effc19de7024ede12621 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 07:37:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] libgame: sprites from a picture, and text from a system font
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Neither art nor letters should have to be arrays in a source file.

DefineSprite() now takes a BBitmap or a path — anything the Translation
Kit reads. At four bits the picture's colours are written into the sprite
palette you name and the sprite indexes that, so loading it is the whole
of setting it up; at eight they are matched to the global palette as it
stands, for a sprite meant to share the world's colours. More distinct
colours than there is room for are matched to the nearest already taken,
so a stray anti-aliased pixel costs a shade and not the sprite.

SetTextFont() renders a system font once into glyph shapes of its own —
any family, any size, and no font data in the program. It takes the first
fixed-width family it finds, since be_fixed_font is not reliably one
here. DrawText() then blits glyphs into the world with everything else,
so text sits in the scene: the overlay filters it, and it costs none of
the 64 sprites a frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Prose-Patch: 0111-libgame-sprites-from-a-picture-and-text-from-a-system-font.patch 8cdd5e11e489e5199bc2dbdb6d41783f40d4e4925c79171ef21e81e644a51df1
